# Java — Comparable vs Comparator

When we sort numbers or strings, Java already knows how to compare them.

But what happens when we create our **own objects**?

For example:

```java
Student s1 = new Student("A", 85);
Student s2 = new Student("B", 72);
Student s3 = new Student("C", 95);
```

How does Java know whether `s1` should come before `s2`?

This is where **Comparable** and **Comparator** come in.

---

# 1. Comparable

`Comparable` is used when a class has a **natural/default ordering**.

A class implements:

```java
Comparable<T>
```

and defines:

```java
compareTo()
```

### Example

```java
class Student implements Comparable<Student> {

    String name;
    int marks;

    Student(String name, int marks) {
        this.name = name;
        this.marks = marks;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Student other) {
        return this.marks - other.marks;
    }
}
```

Now students can be sorted according to their marks.

```java
Collections.sort(students);
```

The comparison is defined **inside the Student class itself**.

---

# 2. What Does `compareTo()` Return?

The method:

```java
compareTo()
```

returns an integer.

```text
Negative → current object comes before other object

Zero → both are considered equal in ordering

Positive → current object comes after other object
```

Example:

```java
return this.marks - other.marks;
```

If:

```text
this.marks = 70
other.marks = 90
```

then:

```text
70 - 90 = -20
```

So the current student comes before the other student.

---

# 3. Complete Comparable Example

```java
import java.util.*;

class Student implements Comparable<Student> {

    String name;
    int marks;

    Student(String name, int marks) {
        this.name = name;
        this.marks = marks;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Student other) {
        return Integer.compare(this.marks, other.marks);
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + " - " + marks;
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();

        students.add(new Student("A", 85));
        students.add(new Student("B", 72));
        students.add(new Student("C", 95));

        Collections.sort(students);

        System.out.println(students);
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
[B - 72, A - 85, C - 95]
```

The `Student` class itself decides that **marks are the natural ordering**.

---

# 4. Comparator

Now suppose we want different ways of sorting.

Maybe we want:

```text
Sort by marks
Sort by name
Sort by age
Sort by ID
```

Should we keep changing `compareTo()`?

No.

This is where **Comparator** is useful.

A `Comparator` allows us to define an **external/custom ordering**.

```java
Comparator<Student> byName =
        (s1, s2) -> s1.name.compareTo(s2.name);
```

Then:

```java
students.sort(byName);
```

---

# 5. Comparator Example

```java
import java.util.*;

class Student {

    String name;
    int marks;

    Student(String name, int marks) {
        this.name = name;
        this.marks = marks;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + " - " + marks;
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();

        students.add(new Student("Rahul", 85));
        students.add(new Student("Ananya", 72));
        students.add(new Student("Zoya", 95));

        Comparator<Student> byName =
                (s1, s2) -> s1.name.compareTo(s2.name);

        students.sort(byName);

        System.out.println(students);
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
[Ananya - 72, Rahul - 85, Zoya - 95]
```

Notice that `Student` does **not** implement `Comparable`.

The sorting logic exists outside the class.

---

# 6. Multiple Comparators

This is where `Comparator` becomes powerful.

We can create different sorting strategies.

### Sort by Name

```java
Comparator<Student> byName =
        (s1, s2) -> s1.name.compareTo(s2.name);
```

### Sort by Marks

```java
Comparator<Student> byMarks =
        (s1, s2) -> Integer.compare(s1.marks, s2.marks);
```

Now:

```java
students.sort(byName);
```

or:

```java
students.sort(byMarks);
```

Same objects.

Different ordering.

---

# 7. Descending Order

We can reverse a comparator.

```java
students.sort(byMarks.reversed());
```

If the marks are:

```text
72
85
95
```

the result becomes:

```text
95
85
72
```

---

# 8. Comparable vs Comparator

| Comparable                          | Comparator                           |
| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Defines natural ordering            | Defines custom ordering              |
| Uses `compareTo()`                  | Uses `compare()`                     |
| Implemented by the class            | Usually separate from the class      |
| Modifies the class                  | Doesn't require modifying the class  |
| Usually one natural ordering        | Can have multiple sorting strategies |
| `Collections.sort(list)` can use it | `list.sort(comparator)` uses it      |

---

# 9. Simple Real-World Example

Imagine an e-commerce application.

You have:

```java
Product
```

with:

```text
name
price
rating
```

There isn't necessarily one correct way to sort products.

Users might want:

```text
Price: Low → High
Price: High → Low
Rating: Highest → Lowest
Name: A → Z
```

Using `Comparator`, we can create each strategy separately.

```java
Comparator<Product> byPrice =
        Comparator.comparingDouble(p -> p.price);

Comparator<Product> byRating =
        Comparator.comparingDouble(p -> p.rating).reversed();

Comparator<Product> byName =
        Comparator.comparing(p -> p.name);
```

Now the same `Product` objects can be sorted in different ways.

---

# 10. Modern Comparator Syntax

Instead of writing:

```java
Comparator<Student> byMarks =
        (s1, s2) -> Integer.compare(s1.marks, s2.marks);
```

Java provides:

```java
Comparator<Student> byMarks =
        Comparator.comparingInt(s -> s.marks);
```

For strings:

```java
Comparator<Student> byName =
        Comparator.comparing(s -> s.name);
```

And we can chain comparisons:

```java
Comparator<Student> comparator =
        Comparator.comparingInt((Student s) -> s.marks)
                  .thenComparing(s -> s.name);
```

This means:

```text
First → compare marks
If marks are equal → compare names
```

---

# 11. The Key Difference

Think about it like this:

```text
Comparable
    ↓
"What is the natural order of MY objects?"

Comparator
    ↓
"How do I want to order these objects THIS TIME?"
```

### Comparable

```java
class Student implements Comparable<Student>
```

The class says:

> "This is how my objects should normally be ordered."

### Comparator

```java
Comparator<Student>
```

An external object says:

> "I'll decide how these objects should be ordered."

---

# 12. Important Interview Point

Don't confuse:

```java
compareTo()
```

with:

```java
equals()
```

They have different purposes.

```text
equals()
    ↓
Are these objects logically equal?

compareTo()
    ↓
What is their ordering relationship?
```

For sorted collections such as `TreeSet` and `TreeMap`, comparison behavior can affect whether elements are considered duplicates for ordering purposes.

So your comparison logic should be designed carefully.

---

# Quick Memory Trick

```text
Comparable
→ compareTo()
→ Natural ordering
→ Inside the class

Comparator
→ compare()
→ Custom ordering
→ Outside the class
→ Multiple ways to sort
```

## Final Takeaway

**Comparable** is best when your class has one clear natural ordering.

**Comparator** is better when you need multiple ways to sort the same objects.

```text
Student
   │
   ├── by marks
   ├── by name
   ├── by age
   └── by ID
        ↓
   Comparator
```

This makes `Comparator` especially useful in real-world applications where users or business requirements may need different sorting strategies.
